olling; the March Ragnar poll is old and partisan-sponsored", "Trone Garriott has slightly outraised the incumbent with comparable cash on hand"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Democratic edge: a strong midterm environment, Trone Garriott's fundraising parity, and a rating consensus tilting toward a flip, with Kalshi and Polymarket both pricing the Democrat near 76%. Nunn's incumbency and the district's Republican lean keep this short of Likely D.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling or a shift in the national environment after the war-powers and Epstein-related coverage could move this toward Tossup or Likely D.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Zach Nunn (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Iowa's 3rd District pairs first-term Republican incumbent Zach Nunn against Democratic state senator Sarah Trone Garriott in a seat with a slight Republican partisan lean. The national midterm environment favors Democrats, and Trone Garriott has kept pace financially, raising about $5.3 million to Nunn's $4.9 million with similar cash on hand. Outside spending has been minimal so far.\n\nMost expert raters call the race a Tossup, though several rate it a Democratic flip, and prediction markets give the Democrat roughly a three-in-four chance. Recent headlines show Nunn breaking with his party on a war-powers vote and facing a complaint over a Hegseth campaign appearance, while Trone Garriott has drawn coverage around the Epstein files exhibit in Des Moines. With no reliable general-election polling, the race sits as a genuine but Democratic-tilted contest.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7301, "margin": 3.78}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7299, "margin": 3.5}]}